Oh, just an interesting note:

"broda poi'a purci" is the correct re=C3=ABxpression for "pu broda". Tense =
in=20
Lojban is aoristic; it happening in the past does not have any implications=
=20
concerning that that action could not have continued into the present of=20
future, or that another event which could be described in otherwise=20
identical fashion cannot be happening (again) in the present or could not=
=20
happen (again) in the future. The restrictive nature of "poi'a" encodes the=
=20
possibility that other versions of what is otherwise the same bridi notion=
=20
exist truthfully. (You got this correct, btw. I am just making an=20
observation, which I find interesting, for the record. It might even become=
=20
handy for future discussions/lessons/introductions - or this one, if you=20
edit it (but I am not suggesting that you should do so)).

This means that we now have a symmetric and easy way to express exclusive=
=20
(non-aoristic) tenses. Before, we would have needed to include "po'o"=20
(example: ~"pu po'o broda") or to use negated AND connectives (example: "pu=
=20
jenai ca jenai ba broda") in order to express this meaning. Now, we can=20
just use "noi'a" (example: "broda noi'a purci").

___

I do worry about the interaction between "noi'a" with a temporal tense and=
=20
other nontemporal tenses. Since space and time are symmetric in Lojbanic=20
treatment, saying that it happened only in the past might mean that it did=
=20
not happen at any spatial location (because the only tense applicable is=20
the past, not any location tense). Likewise, "noi'a purci" (and nothing=20
more) might mean that the event occurred in the past and nothing else,=20
which means that it could not have occurred at any spatial location.=20
Clearly, both of these interpretations are nonsensical because 'events'=20
which occur must have done so both in some nonempty region of space and=20
during some nonempty interval of time. Now, in the "pu po'o" case, I think=
=20
that this is a much more serious (although possibly circumventable)=20
problem; but in the "noi'a purci" case, it might be possible to make some=
=20
sort of argument against it due to the nature of selbri/brivla. But I am=20
not sure. It still seems dangerous to me.

